# Optional advanced overrides. You do NOT need a .env file: the setup wizard (open Kipple in a
# browser after the first start and create your account) creates the
# account and sets the time zone, theme, feeds and the address, and Settings covers the rest. Copy this to .env
# only for something below. Every line is commented out, so an untouched copy changes nothing;
# remove the leading "# " from a line to use it. Never commit .env; only this file is checked in.
# See internal/config/config.go for parsing and defaults.
#
# Two kinds of variable. KIPPLE_ADDR, KIPPLE_DATA and the tuning values are read at every start.
# The others are seeds: they give a setting its first value, for scripted deploys, and are not read
# again once that setting is stored. The account variables and TZ seed the account and the time
# zone; the reachability variables (Cloudflare Access, KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL, KIPPLE_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS,
# KIPPLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS) seed the settings under Settings, Account & Devices, Address and access.
# After that, Settings decides: change them there (they apply at once, no restart). A seed
# variable whose setting already holds a different value is logged at start as not used.

# --- Server ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Address the HTTP server listens on. Default ":1919" (all interfaces inside the container; the
# compose files publish it on 127.0.0.1 only). If the address is taken, Kipple exits with an
# error naming it and this variable. Change the port mapping in your compose file together
# with this value.
# KIPPLE_ADDR=:1919

# Directory holding the SQLite database and other persistent state. Default "/data" (the image
# creates it owned by the nonroot user; the compose files mount a named volume there).
# KIPPLE_DATA=/data

# --- Account (optional: the setup wizard does this) ------------------------

# Create the single account from the environment instead of the browser wizard, for scripted
# deploys. Both must be set: username 1-64 characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -, password 5 to 256
# characters ("change-me" is refused). Used only on the first start with an
# empty database; an account created this way never sees the wizard's onboarding steps. Changing
# them later has no effect: use `kipple password` to reset the password, and remove the lines
# afterwards. With either one missing (a lone KIPPLE_USERNAME is ignored) Kipple starts in setup
# mode and the wizard asks, and an empty KIPPLE_PASSWORD never means "no password".
# KIPPLE_USERNAME=owner
# KIPPLE_PASSWORD=

# Initial Google Reader API (sync client) password, 16 to 256 characters (it guards the public sync
# endpoint). If unset, generate one in the wizard's last step or Settings, or run
# `kipple api-password` (recommended: it makes a random 24-character one). Applied at start only
# while the account has no Reader API password; it never replaces an existing one. A value outside
# that range stops the start that would apply it to a new account.
# KIPPLE_API_PASSWORD=

# --- Address and access (optional seeds: Settings, Account & Devices) ----
#
# Each of these gives its setting a first value on a start where that setting was never stored,
# and is ignored after that (a warning names a variable that disagrees with its setting). Change
# them in Settings, Account & Devices, Address and access; the setup wizard also asks for the
# public URL. A value that is not valid stops the start.

# Cloudflare Access: leave both unset unless Cloudflare Access sits in front of Kipple. Kipple
# never needs Cloudflare. Set both or neither: one without the other stops startup. Setting:
# security.cloudflare_access.
#
# When on, Kipple checks the Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion header Access adds to each request it lets
# through: the RS256 signature against the team's keys at
# https://<team domain>/cdn-cgi/access/certs (cached for an hour), the issuer
# (https://<team domain>), the audience (the AUD below) and the expiry. A verified token never
# replaces the session cookie. It does two things:
# - Settings shows the Access email you are signed in with.
# - The web password becomes optional: the wizard offers "No password, through Cloudflare Access" when
#   you open it through Access, and Settings > Remove web password does the same later (only offered
#   through Access, and it asks for the current password). An account without a password signs in
#   ONLY on requests carrying a verified token, so going around Access (the LAN, a published port)
#   cannot sign in. Anyone your Access policy for this application admits can: keep it to your own
#   email. While the account has no web password, Settings refuses to change or turn off Access
#   (set a password first).
# The Reader API (sync apps) always keeps its own API password.
#
# Team domain: Zero Trust > Settings > Custom Pages, e.g. yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com (https://
# is optional; no path).
# KIPPLE_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com
# Application Audience (AUD) tag: Zero Trust > Access > Applications > your Kipple application >
# Overview.
# KIPPLE_ACCESS_AUD=

# Public URL Kipple is reached at (setting: server.public_url). It is the "+url" part of the fetch
# User-Agent and the base of Reader API iconUrl values (when the greader.icon_urls setting is on).
# OPML exports do not use it. If empty, sync apps get no feed icons. It must be an absolute http(s)
# URL with a host and no query, fragment or user info. Its host is also allowed by the Host check
# below, so a name any device on your network can answer (a single word such as nas, or a .local,
# .lan, .home.arpa or .internal name) is refused when it would be stored. Example:
# https://rss.example.com
# KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL=

# Comma-separated IP addresses and CIDR ranges (192.0.2.10, 198.51.100.0/24, 2001:db8::/32) allowed
# to tell Kipple who the client is (setting: security.trusted_proxies): X-Forwarded-For (the
# rightmost hop that is not itself listed), CF-Connecting-IP when there is no X-Forwarded-For, and
# X-Forwarded-Proto (the Secure cookie flag). The client address keys the web sign-in and sync login
# budgets, which group an IPv6 client by its /64. Set this to the address(es) your reverse proxy /
# tunnel connects FROM as Kipple sees them, e.g. 192.0.2.10 (a placeholder; use your own), and never
# a range wider than your proxies (a range wider than an IPv4 /8 or an IPv6 /32 that covers public addresses, such as 0.0.0.0/0, is refused
# when this is stored as the setting, with what to do). Trust a Docker network (or any range
# clients can reach directly) ONLY when the published port is reachable by the proxy alone:
# otherwise any client in it can write its own X-Forwarded-For / CF-Connecting-IP and be believed. A
# trusted peer also makes open mode refuse the request as "forwarded" (it fails closed). Leave unset
# to always use the TCP peer address. If a request carries one of those headers from a peer that is
# not listed, the header is ignored and Kipple logs a WARN (at most once an hour) naming the peer, so
# a missing entry is easy to spot: without it every visitor shares the proxy's address and one budget
# (wrong passwords are slowed, never counted against anyone else; even one persistent guesser on a
# shared address can make sign-in answer "busy, try again", never a lockout).
# KIPPLE_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# Comma-separated extra host names Kipple answers to during first-run setup and while it runs
# without a password ("open mode"), e.g. rss.example.com or *.example.com (no scheme or port).
# Setting: security.allowed_hosts. This is DNS-rebinding protection: in those two modes a request
# whose Host header is not allowed gets "421 Misdirected Request". Always allowed: IP addresses (so
# http://<ip>:1919 always works), localhost, names ending in .localhost or .ts.net (Tailscale), and
# the host of the public URL. During setup also single-word names such as nas and names ending in
# .local, .lan, .home.arpa or .internal. Open mode answers those only when listed, because any device
# on the local network can answer such names (mDNS, LLMNR, router DHCP names) with this computer's
# address. To use Kipple without a password at a name such as nas.local, open it by its IP address
# and add the name in Settings. With a password (the normal case) other names are only logged, never
# refused.
# KIPPLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# --- Time -----------------------------------------------------------------

# IANA time zone, e.g. America/New_York. Kipple reads it to seed the time zone setting: on a start
# where no time zone has been chosen yet it is stored as the setting. After that the setting (the
# wizard or Settings, no restart needed) decides log timestamps once the database is open, daily
# reading statistics, the nightly 04:10 maintenance and backup file names; changing or removing
# TZ does not move them. (Go's own TZ handling still applies to the first start-up log lines and
# to the CLI subcommands.) UTC until chosen when unset; a restored backup keeps its own zone. An
# unknown name stops a start that would have stored it. time/tzdata is compiled in, so this
# resolves without a filesystem zoneinfo database.
# TZ=America/New_York

# --- Fetch scheduler -----------------------------------------------------

# How often the scheduler checks for feeds due to be polled. Default 30s, minimum 1s.
# KIPPLE_SCHED_TICK=30s

# Number of concurrent fetch worker goroutines. Default 8.
# KIPPLE_FETCH_WORKERS=8

# Max concurrent in-flight fetches per host. Default 2.
# KIPPLE_FETCH_PER_HOST=2

# --- Logging ------------------------------------------------------------

# log/slog level: debug, info, warn or error. Default info.
# KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Set to 1 to also log Google Reader API form values (truncated; the password and tokens are
# always redacted) at debug level with KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=debug. Useful for diagnosing a new sync
# client. Default unset/false.
# KIPPLE_LOG_GREADER_FORMS=

# --- Go runtime -----------------------------------------------------------

# GOMEMLIMIT=64MiB   # set in docker-compose.example.yml, with mem_limit: 256m; a soft
#                    # Go heap limit (SQLite's own heap is outside it)
